1
00:00:01,050 --> 00:00:03,603
At a competition, you cannot watch every
run.

2
00:00:04,551 --> 00:00:06,986
And a score alone cannot tell you what
happened.

3
00:00:07,550 --> 00:00:10,704
Each replay lives beside the team’s
result, so a missed

4
00:00:10,704 --> 00:00:11,942
run can be watched again.

5
00:00:12,517 --> 00:00:14,941
Anyone who missed the event can revisit it
in a browser.

6
00:00:15,638 --> 00:00:18,393
Teams can review their performance without
installing Webots.

7
00:00:19,519 --> 00:00:20,862
This is more than a flat video.

8
00:00:21,647 --> 00:00:24,158
Rotate the scene to examine the run from
another viewpoint.

9
00:00:24,854 --> 00:00:27,941
The event history explains what the robot
achieved, with

10
00:00:27,941 --> 00:00:29,301
its original match timestamp.

11
00:00:30,038 --> 00:00:32,990
Seek forward, and the event display
follows the recording.

12
00:00:36,435 --> 00:00:39,321
Rewind, and the appropriate earlier event
returns.

13
00:00:40,011 --> 00:00:42,626
Final results remain separate from the
replay timeline.

14
00:00:43,327 --> 00:00:44,703
The final score stays unchanged.

15
00:00:45,478 --> 00:00:48,899
Restarting clears later events until those
moments are reached again.

16
00:00:50,774 --> 00:00:54,680
Erebus, the competition simulator,
captures the scene, robot movement,

17
00:00:55,099 --> 00:00:56,860
and match results as a data export.

18
00:00:57,331 --> 00:01:01,535
Now, the organizer opens the workspace,
then Results and recordings.

19
00:01:02,217 --> 00:01:05,229
Notice the empty score and time fields
before uploading.

20
00:01:05,975 --> 00:01:08,228
Choose the recording upload for this team
and round.

21
00:01:09,776 --> 00:01:12,929
The complete ZIP keeps the scene,
animation, and supporting

22
00:01:12,929 --> 00:01:14,793
files together in one package.

23
00:01:15,401 --> 00:01:18,420
After choosing the export, the organizer
saves it once.

24
00:01:19,120 --> 00:01:22,845
The website reads the result and fills
empty score and time fields automatically.

25
00:01:23,322 --> 00:01:26,695
This avoids retyping, while preserving
existing manual entries.

26
00:01:27,393 --> 00:01:30,309
Screen recording can add workload and
larger uploads.

27
00:01:30,811 --> 00:01:32,808
A data export keeps the view interactive.

28
00:01:33,677 --> 00:01:37,687
This filmed demo package is just one point
four megabytes; sizes vary.

29
00:01:39,438 --> 00:01:40,347
Define the round once.

30
00:01:42,865 --> 00:01:46,019
Arrange its teams, run lengths, and gaps,
and the website

31
00:01:46,019 --> 00:01:47,396
calculates their run times.

32
00:01:48,354 --> 00:01:50,170
Those runs appear in the schedule
together.

33
00:01:51,350 --> 00:01:55,213
Moving the round block moves its team
times too, avoiding

34
00:01:55,213 --> 00:01:57,005
separate edits for every entry.

35
00:01:58,456 --> 00:02:00,386
Challenges can use different scoring
methods.

36
00:02:00,946 --> 00:02:05,745
Choose sum, average, best scores, or
normalized averages after discards.

37
00:02:06,523 --> 00:02:10,108
The table applies the selected rule
consistently, including normalized

38
00:02:10,108 --> 00:02:13,850
averages and discarded rounds, instead of
relying on hand calculations.

39
00:02:15,894 --> 00:02:18,053
The field package belongs to the round’s
settings.

40
00:02:18,801 --> 00:02:21,587
Organizers control its release and who can
access it.

41
00:02:22,249 --> 00:02:25,995
Once released, visitors open Maps, choose
a round, and explore

42
00:02:25,995 --> 00:02:27,284
the three-dimensional field.

43
00:02:29,590 --> 00:02:33,129
Rotate the model, read the field facts,
and check its maximum score.

44
00:02:34,633 --> 00:02:37,446
Named file requests keep participant
submissions organized.

45
00:02:38,182 --> 00:02:41,529
For participant files, create a named
request, choose the

46
00:02:41,529 --> 00:02:43,307
teams, and set the upload window.

47
00:02:44,037 --> 00:02:46,458
Browse received files inside that same
request.

48
00:02:47,351 --> 00:02:48,333
Teams submit privately.

49
00:02:49,227 --> 00:02:52,950
Deadlines lock changes, while saved files
and receipts remain accessible

50
00:02:52,950 --> 00:02:54,741
to that team and the organizers.

51
00:02:55,917 --> 00:03:00,060
For organizer materials, prepare one pack
and select its audience.

52
00:03:00,722 --> 00:03:03,838
Searchable team selection makes targeted
delivery straightforward.

53
00:03:04,494 --> 00:03:07,859
Multiple files stay in one record with its
full recipient list.

54
00:03:09,517 --> 00:03:12,947
Teams find approved packs in their own
dashboard, alongside the

55
00:03:12,947 --> 00:03:14,339
information they need to compete.

56
00:03:16,045 --> 00:03:20,454
Announcements use the same audience-based
approach: public updates or selected

57
00:03:20,454 --> 00:03:22,912
teams, with optional personalized email.

58
00:03:23,749 --> 00:03:26,269
One announcement retains the complete
recipient list.

59
00:03:26,850 --> 00:03:31,147
Updates also appear in the team dashboard,
keeping communication close to the work.

60
00:03:32,598 --> 00:03:35,571
Private complaints give questions and
supporting evidence a

61
00:03:35,571 --> 00:03:36,771
clear route to organizers.

62
00:03:39,760 --> 00:03:42,430
Set up the event's dates, details, and
phase.

63
00:03:44,377 --> 00:03:47,470
Manage team contacts and account access in
one place.

64
00:03:48,850 --> 00:03:52,257
SuperTeams pair existing teams into a
shared group.

65
00:03:53,030 --> 00:03:56,262
Members keep their accounts and access
that group’s private workspace.

66
00:03:57,100 --> 00:03:57,727
Relive the run.

67
00:03:58,545 --> 00:03:58,981
Run the event.

68
00:03:59,677 --> 00:04:01,157
Provided by Peyman Yousefi.
